# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF AN INTEGRITY CONTROL HERE, NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.
#
#  computePackageHash() sha256s the RAW BYTES of every file in the execution
#  package. This repo previously had core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes,
#  so git rewrote line endings whenever it materialised a file — and the hash
#  moved for reasons that had nothing to do with content.
#
#  Measured on 2026-08-02: a `git stash push` / `git stash pop` round-trip over
#  five packaged files, with `git diff` showing not one textual change, moved
#  the package hash 167b15cb… -> 57f24d25…, and turned a guard test red because
#  it slices uiaCanary.ps1 on '\n}\n', which stops matching under CRLF.
#
#  An integrity value that moves for reasons unrelated to content trains its
#  readers to wave it through. So: ONE byte sequence, on every machine, in
#  every checkout, forever. LF, chosen because it is what the repository has
#  always stored — CRLF was only ever a checkout artefact.
#
#  BOM: the text/eol attributes convert CR and LF bytes and nothing else. A
#  UTF-8 BOM is EF BB BF, which contains neither, so the BOM-bearing packaged
#  .ps1 files are unaffected. This is asserted, not assumed, by
#  packageHashStability.test.js. PowerShell 5.1 parses LF-only scripts without
#  complaint; the BOM, not the line ending, is what makes it read UTF-8.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Everything text, normalised to LF in the repository and on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The execution package's own types, stated explicitly rather than left to the
# text=auto heuristic — these are the files whose bytes are hashed.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# Documentation and the rest, same rule, so a doc edit can never be the thing
# that makes a checkout differ from another checkout.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
*.example   text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touched, never guessed at. None are tracked today; this is
# here so that adding one cannot silently corrupt it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
